# Snyk (https://snyk.io) policy file

# This policy file is used to configure Snyk for two different types of vulnerability
# scans: static code analysis of our application code, and finding vulnerabilities in
# the libraries that our application depends on (dependency scanning). Both scans are
# performed using the same Snyk CLI tool, with different command line options.

# If outside scans of our code are done against our GitHub repo, this .snyk policy
# will be found and used by those scans as well, and the reports that they generate
# should match the ones generated by local runs of the Snyk CLI tool.

# The overall remediation process is similar for both types of scans, but how you
# record un-remediated vulnerabilities is different for the two types of scans.
# If you're able to fully remediate a vulnerability of either kind, it will no
# longer appear in the reports and there's no need to update this policy file.
# If your remediation is valid, but is not recognized by Snyk, or if you determine
# that a vulnerability is not a risk, you must modify this policy file to exclude
# the check for that vulnerability so that it does not appear in the reports.
# The way that you exclude vulnerabilities using this policy file is different for
# the two types of scans.

# The exclude.global section excludes files and directories from products that
# support file exclusions, such as Snyk Code. The "dist" and "build" directories are
# commonly added here. It does not exclude ordinary dependency (Open Source) projects
# or suppress their vulnerabilities: choose which manifests are scanned to set the
# dependency scope, and use the "ignore" section for approved exceptions.

# You can exclude specific directories for code scans by adding them to the
# exclude.code section. You cannot do the same for dependency scans.
# There is no exclude.dependencies section.

# Code scans should be done only on your application source code. Scanning other
# files or directories in your project is not useful and can take a very, very
# long time to process. For this reason, you will want to have a list of standard
# exclusion directories in the exclude.code section of your policy file. At the
# very least you should exclude your node_modules directory. (Any vulnerabilities
# in node packages should be caught by the dependency scan, instead.)

# If you determine that a dependency vulnerability is not a risk for your application,
# you can ignore it for the application as a whole by adding it to the "ignore"
# section of the policy file. This ignore section does not apply to code
# vulnerabilities.

# Process for dependency vulnerability remediation:
# 1) Run the Snyk CLI command to scan for dependency vulnerabilities.
# 2) For each vulnerability discovered, either fully remediate it, or decide that
#    we can live with the risk.
# 3) If you don't remediate, add the vulnerability to the "ignore" section of the
#    policy file. You must also add a "reason" property to the entry that explains
#    why it is acceptable to assume the risk of not remediating. Follow the pattern
#    you see below to comment each mitigation fully, using the output of the Snyk tool.
# 4) Having updated the policy file, re-run the scan to ensure that the vulnerability
#    does not show up in the audit report.

# Process for code vulnerability remediation:
# 1) Run the Snyk CLI command to scan for code vulnerabilities.
# 2) For each vulnerability discovered, either fully remediate it, or decide that
#    we can live with the risk.
# 3) If you remediated the vulnerability and it no longer shows up in scans. Stop here.
# 4) If you don't remediate, either because the risk is acceptable, or because your
#    remediation is not recognized by Snyk, factor out the offending code into a
#    "mitigated.js" file in the same directory as the original file. If the mitigated.js
#    file already exists, add to it.
# 5) Add the "mitigated.js" file to the "exclude.code" section of the policy file,
#    if it is not already there.
# 6) Document the vulnerability and your reason for either accepting its risk, or
#    explaining how you remediated it. Put this explanation in the "reason" section
#    of your documentation. Follow the pattern you see below to comment each mitigation
#    fully, using the output of the Snyk tool.
# 7) Having updated the policy file, re-run the scan to ensure that the vulnerability
#    does not show up in the audit report.

version: v1.25.0

# Code and dependency scan exclusions
exclude:

  # Global file exclusions for products that support them, such as Snyk Code
  # (not dependency-project exclusions)
  global:
    - dist/**
    - build/**

  # Code scan exclusions
  code:

    # Standard code scan exclusions
    - node_modules/**
    - bin/**
    - test/**
    - tests/**
    - tools/**
    - coverage/**
    - examples/**
    - assets/**
    - vendor/**
    - '**/*.min.js'

    # Project-specific exclusions for non-runtime content (generated files,
    # fixtures, data dumps). Keep runtime code scanned.

    # Application-specific code scan exclusions for mitigated issues

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
